{"id":"c02103f7-ab9b-4f2f-824d-043c023e5ab8","object":"chat.completion","created":1785022939,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(p, []).append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node found')\\n    candidate_ids = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda x: (-node_map[x]['depth'], x))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for nid in candidate_ids:\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(child not in removed_set for child in children):\\n            continue\\n        removed_set.add(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in node_map if nid not in removed_set])\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that processes the given payload. The payload contains nodes (list of objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols (list of symbols). We need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain, based on some algorithm that is described as \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\", \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\", etc. It seems like we need to simulate a reduction process: we traverse nodes deeper first, tentatively remove a node if it's removable, if all required symbols remain represented (i.e., after removal, the set of symbols in the remaining tree still includes all required_symbols). Also, we must never remove root, required symbol, or a node with retained children. And we need to preserve required symbols.\n\nThe output must have removed_node_ids (list of strings, order of removal) and retained_node_ids (list of strings, sorted). The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to output the IDs of nodes that are removed (in order they were removed) and the IDs of nodes that are retained (sorted).\n\nWe must not mutate the input.\n\nFirst, understand the problem: There is a tree of nodes, each with a parent reference (by ID). The root has parent null. We need to consider nodes that are removable (removable: true). We should attempt to remove them in order of deeper nodes first (depth descending), and among same depth, use node ID as tie breaker (presumably alphabetical? Since IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc., but could be arbitrary? The spec says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort by depth descending, then by ID (lexicographic). Then for each candidate, we check if removing it (and its subtree, presumably) preserves the required symbols. Also, we cannot remove a node that has retained children (i.e., if some child is retained, we cannot remove the parent). Also, we cannot remove root or a node whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\nBut careful: The description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we need to decide which nodes are retained/removed during the process. This is a reduction algorithm: we try to remove nodes that are removable, but only if after removal the set of symbols present in the tree (considering that removing a node also removes its entire subtree?) still covers required_symbols. Typically in Perses, removal of a node means removing that node and its entire subtree, but children might be moved up? Actually in parse tree reduction, you delete a node and its children, but the children may be moved to the parent if the node is deletable? The problem statement says \"grammar_valid deletion and replacement candidates\". It mentions \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" I think it's simpler: we consider removing a node entirely (i.e., delete it and its entire subtree). But then the symbol might become missing. Also, we must not remove nodes that have retained children; that suggests that if we decide to retain a child node (i.e., not remove it), then we cannot remove its parent, because that would also remove the child. So the process is: we iterate through nodes in some order, and tentatively decide to remove a node if it is removable, but only if doing so doesn't cause any required symbol to be absent from the tree (i.e., still present in some retained node's symbol). And also if it has no retained children (i.e., all children are also being removed). This is typical bottom-up reduction: you start from leaves, try to remove leaves first, etc.\n\nGiven the fixture payloads: nodes have depth, parent. The required_symbols are [\"root\", \"s1\"]. Note that root symbol is \"root\". So root must always be retained. Also, any node with symbol \"s1\" must be retained? Actually we need to preserve required symbols, meaning after removal, the set of symbols from retained nodes must include all required_symbols. So we cannot remove all nodes with symbol \"s1\". But we can remove some \"s1\" nodes as long as at least one remains.\n\nThe algorithm: Traverse nodes in order of depth descending (deepest first), and for same depth, sort by node ID (string). For each node, if it is removable (removable==true), and its symbol is not in required_symbols, and it has no retained children (meaning all children in the original tree that are currently decided as retained? Actually we need to decide retention/removal dynamically. Since we process in bottom-up order, we can decide: initially assume all nodes are retained (since we haven't removed anything). Then for each candidate node (in order), we check if we can remove it. Conditions:\n- Node is not root (but root's removable is false anyway)\n- Node's symbol is not in required_symbols\n- None of its children are retained (i.e., all children have been removed or are being removed? Since we process bottom-up, children are processed before parent. So when we consider a node, we have already decided for all its descendants (which are deeper). If any of its children were retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove this node because it would remove a retained child. So condition: For each child of this node (in original tree), that child must be in removed_node_ids (i.e., already removed) or we will later remove it? But since we process children before parent, if a child is removable and we decided to remove it, then it's in removed list. If a child is not removable (removable=false) or its symbol is required, we likely retain it. So we need to track which nodes have been removed so far.\n- After removal, the set of symbols from all retained nodes (including nodes not yet considered? Actually all nodes that are retained (i.e., not removed) must still include all required_symbols. Since we haven't considered nodes above, but they are still retained, the required symbols should be present in the nodes that are retained. We need to check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to be absent. That is, is there at least one remaining node (including nodes not yet visited) that has that required symbol? Since we are removing this node and its subtree, but its subtree may contain required symbols. If all nodes with a particular required symbol are in the subtree of this node (and we are removing this node and thus all those nodes), then after removal that required symbol would be missing.