s, then the count for that symbol would become count-1. If count-1 == 0, we cannot remove. If node's symbol is not required, then counts unchanged, so it's fine.\n\nBut also, we must ensure that we don't remove a node that has retained children. Since we process bottom-up and only remove if all children are removed, this condition is satisfied if we track that children are in removed set.\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Parse case nodes into dict id->node_info (including parent, children list, depth, removable, symbol).\n2. Build mapping from parent to children.\n3. Compute initial symbol counts from all nodes.\n4. Initialize set removed = set().\n5. Get list of node IDs sorted by depth descending, then by ID string (tie break).\n6. For each node_id in that list:\n   - node = data[node_id]\n   - If not node['removable']: skip.\n   - If node['symbol'] in required_symbols: skip (cannot remove required symbol nodes? Actually required_symbols are symbols that must be present, but nodes with those symbols can be removed if there is another node with that symbol. However, the spec says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It says \"a required symbol\" meaning a node whose symbol is required? That implies we cannot remove any node that has a symbol in required_symbols. That is a stronger condition: we cannot remove any node with a required symbol, even if there are other nodes with that symbol. Let's read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"a required symbol\" likely means a node whose symbol is one of the required symbols. So we must never remove a node that carries a required symbol. That simplifies things. So we skip all nodes with symbol in required_symbols.\n   - Check if node has any children that are not in removed set (i.e., children that are still retained). If any child is not removed, skip.\n   - Check if node_id is 'root' (though root's removable is false anyway).\n   - If all conditions satisfied, we tentatively remove it. But we also need to ensure that removing it doesn't cause required symbols to be missing (but since we never remove required symbol nodes, the only required symbols are root and s1, and root is never removed, and s1 nodes are never removed because we skip them, so the condition is automatically satisfied? Wait: The required symbols are \"root\" and \"s1\". We skip nodes with symbol \"s1\", so they are never removed. So the only required symbol that could be missing is \"s1\", but we never remove any s1 node. However, what if all s1 nodes are in a subtree that we remove? But we cannot remove a node that has a child with symbol s1 that is not removed. Since we skip s1 nodes, they are retained. And we check that children are all removed before removing parent. So if an s1 node is a child, the parent cannot be removed because that child (s1) is not removed. So s1 nodes will always be retained because they are never removed and their ancestors may be removed only if the s1 node is not a descendant. But if the s1 node is a descendant, the ancestor cannot be removed because the child (s1) is retained. So s1 symbols will always be present as long as there is at least one s1 node that is not removed. Since we never remove any s1 node, they are all retained. So the required symbols condition is automatically satisfied by the rule \"never remove a required symbol node\". So the required_symbols check becomes unnecessary? But the spec also says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" meaning nodes that have a required symbol. So we just skip those nodes.\n\nThus the algorithm is:\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then node ID.\n- For each node in that order that is removable, not root, not required symbol, and all its children are already removed (since we process bottom-up, children will be processed earlier, so they will be in removed set if they were removable and removed), then we remove it. Add to removed_node_ids (in removal order).\n- After processing all nodes, retained_node_ids are all node IDs not in removed_node_ids, sorted (presumably alphabetically? The spec says \"sorted retained IDs\" – unclear order, but likely sorted ascending by ID string). We'll sort them.\n- Return dict with removed_node_ids (list in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list).\n\nEdge cases: The root has removable false, so it's never removed. Also, nodes with removable false are never candidates.\n\nCheck the fixture cases. Let's manually apply to case 0 to see if this makes sense.\n\nCase 0 payload:\nnodes:\nroot: id=root, parent null, depth 0, removable false\nn1: parent root, depth 1, removable false, symbol s1\nn2: parent root, depth 1, removable true, symbol s2\nn3: parent root, depth 1, removable false, symbol s0\nn4: parent n2, depth 2, removable true, symbol s1 -> but s1 is required, so we skip? Actually n4 symbol is s1, so it is a required symbol node, so we never remove it.\nn5: parent n3, depth 2, removable false, symbol s2\nn6: parent n1, depth 2, removable true, symbol s0\n\nRequired symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]\n\nNodes sorted by depth descending, then ID:\ndepth 2: n4, n5, n6 -> sorted by ID: n4, n5, n6\ndepth 1: n1, n2, n3 -> sorted: n1, n2, n3\ndepth 0: root\n\nProcess:\nn4: removable true, but symbol s1 is required, so skip.\nn5: removable false, skip.\nn6: removable true, symbol s0 not required. Check children: n6 has no children (depth 2 leaves). All children (none) are removed trivially. So remove n6. Add to removed: [\"n6\"]. Update symbol counts? Not needed.\nn1: removable false, skip.\nn2: removable true, symbol s2 not required. Children: n4 (still retained because we skipped it? n4 is not removed because we skipped it. So n2 has child n4 that is not removed. Therefore cannot remove n2 because it has retained children.\nn3: removable false, skip.\nroot: skip.\n\nThus removed = [\"n6\"].\nRetained = all others: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted IDs: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\"